      "text": "Example : Speaker wires.",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-6-s0002",
      "page": 6,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-6-r0005"
      ],
      "text": "Suppose you want to connect your stereo to remote speakers. (a) If each wire must be 20 m long, what diameter copper wire should you use to keep the resistance less than 0.10 Ω per wire? (b) If the current to each speaker is 4.0 A, what is the potential difference, or voltage drop, across each wire?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "simple",
      "detailed_figure_description": null,
      "figure_alternative": "A stereo receiver connected to two remote speakers by separate copper-wire runs, each labeled 20 m.",
      "id": "page-6-s0003",
      "page": 6,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-6-r0003"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-6-s0004",
      "normalized_math": "\\text{(b)}\\quad V=IR=(4.0\\,\\mathrm{A})(0.10\\,\\Omega),\\qquad V_R=0.400\\,\\mathrm{V}=400\\,\\mathrm{mV}",
      "page": 6,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-6-r0004"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Part b. Voltage equals current times resistance, which equals 4.0 amperes times 0.10 ohm. The voltage drop V sub R is 0.400 volt, or 400 millivolts.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "simple",
      "detailed_figure_description": null,
      "figure_alternative": "Circuit sketch of a voltage source connected in series to the resistance of the wiring and a speaker. Arrows labeled I indicate clockwise conventional current.",
      "id": "page-6-s0005",
      "page": 6,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-6-r0006"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-6-s0006",
      "normalized_math": "\\text{(a)}\\quad \\rho_{\\mathrm{Cu}}=1.68\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathrm{m},\\quad L=20\\,\\mathrm{m},\\quad R=0.10\\,\\Omega;\\qquad A=\\pi r^2=\\pi\\left(\\frac d2\\right)^2;\\qquad R=\\rho\\frac LA=\\frac{4\\rho L}{\\pi d^2};\\qquad d^2>\\frac{4\\rho L}{\\pi R}\\Rightarrow d>\\left[\\frac{4\\rho L}{\\pi R}\\right]^{1/2}=\\left[\\frac{4(1.68\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathrm{m})(20\\,\\mathrm{m})}{\\pi(0.10\\,\\Omega)}\\right]^{1/2}=2.1\\,\\mathrm{mm}",
      "page": 6,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-6-r0007",
        "page-6-r0008"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Part a. The resistivity of copper is 1.68 times 10 to the negative 8 ohm meters, the length is 20 meters, and the resistance is 0.10 ohm. Area equals pi r squared, or pi times d over 2 squared. Resistance equals rho L over A, which equals 4 rho L over pi d squared. Therefore d squared must exceed 4 rho L over pi R, so d must exceed the square root of that quantity. Substitution gives 2.1 millimeters.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-6-s0007",
      "level": 1,
      "page": 6,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-6-r0009"
      ],
      "text": "Electrical Energy & Power",
